{# Task rules for the default sequential mode, appended to the framing
   in the task call's system prompt.

   Supervisor voice (2026-09-10, prompt revision v2, plan
   docs/history/plans/2026-09-10-supervisor-voice-prompt-revision.md):
   the banner heading "Signal Task" and its row-of-equals-signs footer
   are gone, every rule sentence is spoken as ``LABEL: sentence``, "turn"
   became "round" (plan §1 A1) and the evaluator vocabulary is
   neutralised -- "the rule", not "the hidden rule" (plan §1 E2).

   Facts are unchanged: the same attribute lists, the same action list,
   the same per-difficulty rule format with its parenthetical, and the
   same few-shot block whenever ``few_shot_examples`` is non-empty.

   Variables:
     colors_str / shapes_str / numbers_str / actions_str : str
     difficulty : str -- selects the rule format shown
     few_shot_examples : list[str] -- already indented "sig → action"
                         lines, empty when the season shows none
#}THE TASK: Each round you see ONE signal. Give the action the rule assigns to it. You work the rule out from the feedback you get after each round.
A SIGNAL has three attributes.
  Color: [{{ colors_str }}]
  Shape: [{{ shapes_str }}]
  Number: [{{ numbers_str }}]
ACTIONS: [{{ actions_str }}]
THE RULE maps certain attribute values to the correct action. It takes one of these forms:
{% if difficulty == "easy" %}  "If <attribute> is <value> then <action>, otherwise <default_action>."
  (The rule depends on a single attribute.)
{% elif difficulty == "medium" %}  "If <attribute> is <value> then <action>, otherwise <default_action>."
  (The rule depends on a single attribute.)
{% elif difficulty == "hard" %}  "If <attr_1> is <val_1> AND <attr_2> is <val_2> then <action_A>; if only <attr_1> is <val_1> then <action_B>; otherwise <default_action>."
  (The rule depends on two attributes with partial-match branching.)
{% elif difficulty == "expert" %}  "If your previous action was correct then <override_action>; otherwise follow this rule: If <attr_1> is <val_1> AND <attr_2> is <val_2> then <action_A>; if only <attr_1> is <val_1> then <action_B>; otherwise <default_action>."
  (The rule depends on two attributes, and your previous round's correctness can override the action.)
{% endif %}{% if few_shot_examples %}EXAMPLES, all following the rule:
{% for example in few_shot_examples %}{{ example }}
{% endfor %}Use them to work out the rule and choose the correct action.
{% endif -%}
